generation.text:
31 CFR § 1020.320. 

This section requires a bank to file a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) if it knows, suspects, or has reason to suspect that a transaction involves or aggregates to more than $5,000 in funds or other assets into or out of, or through the United States, by, through, or in the name of any person, and that knowledge, suspicion, or reason to suspect is based upon one or more of the following:

1. The transaction has no business or apparent lawful purpose or is not the sort in the ordinary course of business of the correspondent bank or one of its customers;
2. The transaction involves the movement of funds or other assets into or out of, or through the United States, by, through, or in the name of any person whose activities, to the bank's knowledge, are subject to a U.S. or foreign law or regulation that requires or has triggered a filing of a suspicious transaction report with a government agency;
3. The transaction involves the movement of funds or other assets into or out of, or through the United States, by, through, or in the name of any person who is the subject of a U.S. or foreign investigation, prosecution, civil enforcement action, or administrative action concerning possible violation of law or regulation;
4. The transaction has no reasonable explanation, low value, and an unexplained transaction is an amount of $5,000 or less; or
5. The transaction involves the movement of funds or other assets into or out of, or through the United States, by, through, or in the name of any person who is the subject of a U.S. or foreign investigation, prosecution, civil enforcement action, or administrative action concerning possible violation of law or regulation.

Note: The $5,000 threshold is an aggregate amount, meaning that multiple transactions below $5,000 can still trigger a SAR if they aggregate to more than $5,000.

